\subsection{Threat Model}

We consider probabilistic polynomial-time (PPT) adversaries that
attempt to infer sensor or query information, violate access
policies, recover aggregate plaintexts, or omit, modify, fabricate,
and replay query responses.

\subsubsection{Honest-but-Curious Edge Gateway}

The Edge Gateway faithfully authenticates sensor packets, constructs
the range index and protected bitmap blocks, registers aggregation
metadata, and anchors signed epoch commitments. It may nevertheless
infer information from its permitted view, including sensor
identities, update times, searchable dimensions, canonical-node
memberships, and bitmap positions. The gateway holds
$K_{\mathrm{sel}}$ but possesses neither the ABSE master secret,
user attribute keys, record-specific decryption keys, nor any
Threshold EC-ElGamal share. It therefore cannot decrypt outsourced
records or aggregate ciphertexts.

The gateway is assumed not to collude with the cloud. Such collusion
could reveal protected bitmap contents and access patterns, although
record and aggregate plaintexts would remain cryptographically
protected. A gateway that deliberately constructs or anchors an
incorrect index is outside the threat model; verification guarantees
are therefore relative to the gateway-committed state.

\subsubsection{Malicious Cloud Server}

The cloud is privacy-curious and potentially malicious with respect
to query execution. It observes encrypted records, ABSE tokens,
protected bitmap blocks, aggregation metadata, access patterns, and
the leakage specified by $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{BVCRSA}}$. It may also
omit matching nodes, modify protected blocks, substitute aggregation
entries, return incorrect aggregates, or replay a previous response.

Such deviations are detected through signed blockchain commitments,
index and bitmap Merkle proofs, aggregation multi-proofs, epoch- and
query-bound tokens, and independent homomorphic recomputation. The
cloud cannot decrypt sensor records or aggregates, generate tokens
for unsatisfied policies, or forge a gateway-authenticated epoch
state except with negligible probability. Availability against a
cloud that refuses to respond is not guaranteed.

\subsubsection{Unauthorized and Colluding Users}

Unauthorized users may attempt to generate valid ABSE tokens, recover
unauthorized node or record keys, replay valid tokens, or collude with
other users and the cloud. ABSE keys are assumed collusion-resistant;
thus, independently issued keys cannot be combined to satisfy a
policy that no colluding user satisfies individually. Query tokens
are bound to the finalized epoch, query identifier, and expiration
time to limit substitution and replay.

\subsubsection{Threshold Decryption Authorities}

Each TDA holds one Threshold EC-ElGamal share. The adversary may
corrupt fewer than $t$ of the $n$ TDAs and collude with the cloud or
users. Such a coalition cannot reconstruct the aggregation secret key
or decrypt aggregate ciphertexts. Malformed partial decryptions are
detected using DLEQ proofs. Collusion among at least $t$ TDAs and TDA
refusal attacks are outside the scope of this work.

\subsubsection{Blockchain and Deployment Assumptions}

The permissioned blockchain is assumed to provide authenticated
access, consensus finality, and immutable finalized states. The user
accepts only the epoch bound to the query and verifies its gateway
signature, timestamp, and validity period. Consequently, modification
or rollback of a finalized query state requires breaking the
blockchain finality assumption, the signature scheme, or the
collision resistance of the hash function.

The TA is trusted during initialization to generate parameters,
issue credentials, and distribute threshold shares, but does not
participate after setup. Cryptographic primitives are assumed secure,
nonces are not reused, and protocol messages are exchanged through
authenticated channels. Compromise of the TA or sensors, a malicious
Edge Gateway, collusion between the gateway and cloud, corruption of
at least $t$ TDAs, rewriting finalized blockchain states, and
physical, side-channel, or denial-of-service attacks are outside the
scope of this work.

