authenticated bitmap completeness and query-state verification.


\subsection{Storage Overhead Analysis}

BVCRSA stores encrypted sensor records, authenticated
range-cover indexes, protected bitmaps, aggregation
ciphertexts, and blockchain commitments. Let $N$ denote the
number of outsourced records and $m_c$ the total number of
canonical interval nodes. The encrypted sensor records incur
$O(N)$ storage, while the authenticated range-cover index
requires $O(m_c)$ searchable-node ciphertexts and Merkle-tree
leaf commitments. Protected bitmaps require
$O(m_cN)$ bits because each canonical node maintains a
fixed-length bitmap over the indexed record space.
Aggregation metadata require one EC-ElGamal ciphertext per
record, yielding $O(N)$ additional storage. Finally, the
blockchain stores only compact epoch-level commitments
consisting of authenticated Merkle roots and signatures,
resulting in $O(E)$ on-chain storage for $E$ finalized
epochs. Consequently, the storage overhead is dominated by
the bitmap index, while blockchain storage remains
independent of the outsourced dataset size.
\subsection{Performance Evaluation}

We evaluate BVCRSA in terms of efficiency, scalability,
privacy-preserving aggregation, and authenticated
verification over large-scale outsourced IIoT data. The
evaluation considers four aspects: (i) searchable-index
construction, (ii) trapdoor generation and conjunctive range
query processing, (iii) encrypted aggregation, and (iv)
blockchain-based query-state verification.

BVCRSA is compared with representative searchable
encryption and range-query schemes, including
Scheme~\cite{ref25}, Trinity~\cite{ref26},
ABSE-Range~\cite{ref27}, Latt-IBEKS~\cite{ref28}, and
VC-KASE~\cite{ref16}. Since no existing scheme simultaneously supports
attribute-based searchable encryption, conjunctive range
queries, encrypted aggregation, authenticated
blockchain-backed verification, and threshold aggregate
decryption, only the functionalities shared by the
compared schemes (e.g., index construction, trapdoor
generation, query processing, and verification) are
evaluated comparatively. The encrypted aggregation
capability of BVCRSA, which is unsupported by the
baseline schemes, is evaluated separately through a
dedicated experiment. The threshold aggregate-decryption
cost is excluded from the comparative evaluation because
it is a post-processing operation not provided by the
baseline schemes.
\subsubsection*{\textbf{Experimental Setup}}

A BVCRSA prototype was implemented in Python~3.12 using
widely adopted open-source cryptographic libraries.
Attribute-based searchable encryption was implemented using
\texttt{py\_ecc} over the BN128 bilinear curve, while
Threshold EC-ElGamal was realized on the NIST P-256
elliptic curve. AES-GCM encryption was provided by
\texttt{PyCryptodome}, and SHA-256 and HMAC were
implemented using Python's native cryptographic libraries.
Blockchain-assisted query-state verification was realized
through Solidity smart contracts deployed on a private
Ethereum test network and accessed via \texttt{Web3.py}.
The blockchain employed the Clique Proof-of-Authority
(PoA) consensus protocol with a 2-s block interval, and
query-state verification accepted only the latest finalized
epoch commitment after one block confirmation, consistent
with the permissioned consortium environment considered by
BVCRSA. All baseline schemes were independently
reimplemented according to their original algorithmic
descriptions and evaluated on the same hardware platform,
software environment, and comparable security settings to
ensure a fair comparison.

Experiments were conducted in a heterogeneous IIoT
environment comprising a Raspberry Pi~4 Model~B (4~GB RAM)
for sensor-side operations, an Intel Core~i7 laptop (16~GB
RAM) for trapdoor generation, and an Intel Xeon server
(32~GB RAM, Ubuntu~22.04~LTS) for index construction,
encrypted query processing, homomorphic aggregation, and
verification.

Synthetic IIoT datasets consisting of records
\(D_i=(m_i,k_i,t_i,v_i)\) were generated using 20 sensor
categories, with numerical values uniformly distributed in
\([0,100]\). The database size varied from
\(10^3\) to \(2\times10^4\) records. Performance was
evaluated with respect to database size
(\(N=10^3\)--\(2\times10^4\)), query selectivity
(10\%--80\%), and the number of queried dimensions
(\(d=1\)--5). The selected query selectivity range
represents a broad spectrum of practical IIoT workloads,
covering both highly selective monitoring queries and
coarse-grained analytical queries. Measured metrics
included index construction, trapdoor generation, query
processing, verification, aggregation, and storage
overhead. Unless otherwise specified, all results represent
the average of 20 independent runs. Since the observed
run-to-run variance was negligible, only average values are
reported.

BVCRSA targets heterogeneous IIoT deployments in which
resource-constrained sensors perform only data acquisition,
symmetric encryption, and authenticated metadata
generation, whereas computationally intensive operations,
including ABSE processing, authenticated index
construction, homomorphic aggregation, and query
verification, are offloaded to the Edge Gateway and cloud.
Accordingly, the evaluation emphasizes the cryptographic
performance and system-level scalability of verifiable
encrypted query processing rather than device-level
resource utilization.

For a dataset containing \(N\) records with sensor values in
\([0,V_{\max}]\), the maximum aggregation count and sum are
\(Count_{\max}=N\) and \(Sum_{\max}=NV_{\max}\),
respectively. Consequently, bounded BSGS requires
\(O(\sqrt{\max(N,NV_{\max})})\) time and memory. Under
our experimental configuration (\(N\le2\times10^4\) and
\(V_{\max}=100\)), the maximum aggregation count and sum
are \(2\times10^4\) and \(2\times10^6\), respectively,
requiring approximately
\(\sqrt{2\times10^6}\approx1.4\times10^3\) baby-step
entries and a comparable number of giant-step iterations.

