\subsubsection{\textbf{Query Processing Time}}

Query-processing latency is a critical performance metric for outsourced searchable-encryption systems because it directly determines the responsiveness of encrypted data retrieval and analytics. This experiment evaluates the query-processing performance of BVCRSA under varying query selectivities, database sizes, and numbers of queried dimensions. The reported query-processing time includes encrypted search, bitmap filtering, and homomorphic aggregation, but excludes network communication and blockchain-access latency to isolate the efficiency of the search framework itself.

Figure~\ref{fig:combined_3_panel} (b) evaluates the effect of
range-query width on query-processing latency. As the queried
interval expands from $10\%$ to $80\%$ of the searchable
domain, all schemes exhibit increasing latency because wider
ranges match more index entries and records, resulting in
additional search, filtering, and aggregation operations.

BVCRSA consistently achieves the lowest query-processing
latency, increasing only from approximately $0.04$~ms to
$0.20$~ms. This modest growth is attributed to the
authenticated hierarchical range-cover index, which limits
search to a logarithmic number of canonical nodes, together
with word-parallel bitmap operations that efficiently merge
matched nodes and evaluate conjunctive predicates.
Although wider query ranges return more bitmap positions and
aggregation entries, bitmap filtering substantially prunes
non-matching records before Threshold EC-ElGamal
aggregation, allowing the processing cost to grow gradually
with query selectivity.

In contrast, Trinity~\cite{ref26}, ABSE-Range~\cite{ref27},
Latt-IBEKS~\cite{ref28}, and VC-KASE~\cite{ref16} exhibit
higher latency because their search procedures require more
computationally intensive cryptographic operations or less
efficient index evaluation as the query range expands.
Overall, the results demonstrate that BVCRSA scales
efficiently with increasing query selectivity while maintaining
low query-processing overhead for multidimensional encrypted
IIoT analytics.

Figure~\ref{fig:combined_3_panel} (c) illustrates the scalability
of query processing as the number of outsourced records
increases from 1,000 to 100,000. All schemes incur higher
latency because larger datasets require more search and
filtering operations.

BVCRSA consistently achieves the lowest latency, increasing
only from approximately $0.02$~ms to $0.45$~ms. This
efficiency stems from the authenticated hierarchical
range-cover index and the $O(N/w)$ word-parallel bitmap
intersection algorithm, which efficiently filters candidate
records without scanning all ciphertexts. Consequently, the
processing cost grows gradually with the database size.

In contrast, Trinity~\cite{ref26}, ABSE-Range~\cite{ref27},
Latt-IBEKS~\cite{ref28}, and VC-KASE~\cite{ref16} incur
higher latency due to more expensive cryptographic operations
or document-dependent search procedures. Overall, the results
demonstrate that BVCRSA scales efficiently for large-scale
encrypted IIoT analytics.

