canonical nodes, while the $O(N/w)$ word-parallel bitmap
filtering processes multiple candidate records
simultaneously. Consequently, query throughput degrades only
slightly as the workload increases.

In contrast, Trinity~\cite{ref26} and
ABSE-Range~\cite{ref27} suffer greater throughput reduction
due to repeated cryptographic matching and index-processing
operations for each query. Latt-IBEKS~\cite{ref28} and
VC-KASE~\cite{ref16} achieve higher throughput than these
schemes but remain limited by lattice-based computations and
pairing-based ciphertext testing, respectively. Overall, the
results demonstrate that BVCRSA sustains high throughput
under heavy query workloads, making it suitable for
large-scale outsourced IIoT analytics.

\subsubsection{\textbf{Verification Overhead}}

This experiment evaluates the cost of verifying search-query results. The number of returned results is varied from $50$ to $500$, while the database size and query parameters remain fixed. The comparison includes BVCRSA, Trinity~\cite{ref26}, and VC-KASE~\cite{ref16}, since these schemes provide explicit result-verification mechanisms.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.78\linewidth]{fig_verification_overhead.png}
\caption{Verification time versus number of returned results.}
\label{fig:verification_overhead}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:verification_overhead} compares the verification overhead of BVCRSA, Trinity~\cite{ref26}, and VC-KASE~\cite{ref16} under varying result-set sizes, with runs interleaved across all three schemes so that each is measured under comparable system conditions at every point in the sweep. BVCRSA verifies returned results through a Merkle multi-proof over the authenticated aggregation entries, in which internal-node hashes shared by multiple selected positions are computed once and reused rather than recomputed along an independent root-to-leaf path for every result; verification latency grows from approximately $0.44$~ms to $1.81$~ms as the number of returned results increases from $50$ to $500$. Trinity~\cite{ref26} performs a lighter per-result check, decrypting and checking a compact per-result encrypted verify array rather than a bare integrity-tag comparison, consistent with Trinity-II's published verification design; because this check carries no tree-structure overhead, Trinity attains the lowest verification latency across the evaluated range, growing from $0.20$~ms to $1.43$~ms. Nevertheless, because Trinity provides no batched or amortized verification mechanism, its cost still scales linearly with $|R_Q|$, so the relative gap between Trinity and BVCRSA narrows as the result-set size grows. VC-KASE~\cite{ref16} maintains a largely constant verification latency of approximately $1.8$~ms once $|R_Q|$ exceeds $250$ (decreasing from an initial $2.25$~ms at $|R_Q|=50$), since its verification cost is dominated by a fixed number of bilinear-pairing operations rather than by $|R_Q|$; consequently, VC-KASE incurs the highest verification cost for small-to-moderate result sets and is marginally overtaken by BVCRSA's growing cost only at the largest evaluated result-set size, $|R_Q|=500$. These results indicate that BVCRSA's batched Merkle-proof verification scales more gracefully with the result-set size than an unbatched per-record approach, while Trinity's lightweight flat-cost check remains the fastest option across the tested range.

\subsubsection{\textbf{Effect of Homomorphic Aggregation}}

This experiment evaluates the scalability benefit of the proposed bitmap-constrained homomorphic aggregation mechanism. The number of matched records is varied from 100 to 500 while the query configuration remains fixed. We compare the full BVCRSA framework against a Naive baseline that returns all matched encrypted records to the user without performing in-network aggregation.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.78\linewidth]{fig_ablation_aggregation.png}
\caption{Effect of homomorphic aggregation versus naive record retrieval.}
\label{fig:ablation_aggregation}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:ablation_aggregation} shows that the Naive approach incurs rapidly increasing latency as the number of matched records grows because all encrypted records must be transmitted and subsequently processed by the user. The execution time increases from approximately 180~ms to nearly 900~ms when the number of matched records reaches 500.

In contrast, BVCRSA performs aggregation directly over encrypted metadata maintained within the authenticated range-cover structure. Consequently, only compact aggregated ciphertexts and verification proofs are returned, substantially reducing communication and processing overhead. As a result, the execution time remains below 20~ms across all tested workloads and exhibits only a modest increase as the match volume grows.

These results demonstrate that the proposed homomorphic aggregation mechanism significantly improves query scalability while preserving data confidentiality and verifiability, making BVCRSA suitable for large-scale IIoT analytics workloads.


\subsubsection{\textbf{Aggregation Strategy Comparison}}

This experiment evaluates the efficiency of BVCRSA's
aggregate-then-decrypt strategy compared with the
conventional decrypt-then-aggregate approach. Both
strategies operate on the same set of matched ciphertexts
using identical $(t,n)=(3,5)$ Threshold EC-ElGamal
decryption over NIST P-256. The number of matched records
is varied from
$|R_Q|=\{10,50,100,500,1{,}000,5{,}000,10{,}000\}$ within a
database containing $N=10{,}000$ encrypted records, and all
results are averaged over 20 independent runs.

In the conventional strategy, every matched ciphertext is
individually threshold-decrypted before computing the final
aggregate. In contrast, BVCRSA first homomorphically
aggregates all matched ciphertexts at the cloud using
Threshold EC-ElGamal and subsequently decrypts only the
aggregated \emph{SUM} and \emph{COUNT} ciphertexts.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\textwidth]{fig_agg_strategy_comparison.png}
\caption{Aggregation strategy comparison.
(a) Number of threshold-decryption operations.
(b) Aggregation latency (log scale).
(c) Aggregation latency for small result sets.}
\label{fig:agg_strategy_comparison}
\end{figure*}

Figure~\ref{fig:agg_strategy_comparison}(a) compares the
number of threshold decryptions required by both strategies.
The conventional approach performs one threshold decryption
per matched record, causing the decryption workload to grow
linearly with $|R_Q|$. In contrast, BVCRSA always performs
only two decryptions, regardless of the aggregation size,
because all matched ciphertexts are first combined through
homomorphic EC-point additions.

This difference directly translates into the latency shown in
Fig.~\ref{fig:agg_strategy_comparison}(b). As the number of
matched records increases from 10 to 10,000, the
conventional strategy grows from 316.8~ms to 196.1~s,
whereas BVCRSA increases only from 63.5~ms to
292.4~ms. The gap widens with increasing aggregation size
because homomorphic ciphertext aggregation introduces only
lightweight EC-point additions, while threshold decryption
remains constant. Consequently, BVCRSA achieves up to a
$670.5\times$ reduction in aggregation latency at the largest
evaluated workload.

Figure~\ref{fig:agg_strategy_comparison}(c) further examines
small aggregation workloads. Although the conventional
strategy is marginally faster when only a single ciphertext is
aggregated, the performance crossover occurs at
approximately $|R_Q|\approx2$. Beyond this point, the
benefit of reducing thousands of threshold decryptions to only
two rapidly outweighs the additional homomorphic
aggregation cost. These results demonstrate that the proposed
aggregate-then-decrypt strategy provides significantly better
scalability for practical IIoT analytics, where aggregation
typically involves many matched records.

\subsubsection{\textbf{Aggregate-Recovery Scalability}}

This experiment evaluates the scalability of aggregate
plaintext recovery using the bounded Baby-Step Giant-Step
(BSGS) algorithm employed after Threshold EC-ElGamal
decryption. The maximum aggregate value
$M_{\max}$ is varied from $10^3$ to $10^7$, and the
results are compared with a conventional linear-search
recovery method. Each measurement is averaged over
20 independent runs.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.78\linewidth]{bsgs_scalability.png}
\caption{Aggregate-recovery latency versus the maximum
supported aggregate value.}
\label{fig:bsgs}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:bsgs} shows that the recovery latency of
BSGS increases gradually with $M_{\max}$, following the
expected $O(\sqrt{M_{\max}})$ complexity. In contrast,
linear search grows approximately as $O(M_{\max})$ and
rapidly becomes impractical as the plaintext domain
expands. For the evaluated IIoT workload, the configured
maximum aggregate value remains well within the BSGS
search range, making aggregate recovery only a small
fraction of the overall query-processing time. These
results confirm that bounded BSGS enables practical
Threshold EC-ElGamal aggregation for large-scale
encrypted IIoT analytics.

\section{Discussion}
Although BVCRSA provides secure and verifiable encrypted
IIoT analytics, several practical considerations remain.

First, BVCRSA is designed primarily for append-dominant
IIoT workloads. Dynamic updates require reconstructing only
the affected authenticated range-cover nodes, bitmap
commitments, and Merkle paths. While these operations are
localized, extremely high update rates may still increase
maintenance overhead.

Second, BVCRSA anchors only compact epoch commitments on
the blockchain instead of searchable indexes or encrypted
sensor data. Consequently, on-chain storage grows with the
number of finalized epochs rather than the dataset size.
However, deployments with very short epoch durations may
still incur increasing blockchain storage and
synchronization costs over time.

Third, BVCRSA adopts a hierarchical architecture in which
resource-constrained sensors perform only data acquisition,
symmetric encryption, and authenticated metadata
generation, while the Edge Gateway and cloud execute
computationally intensive indexing and query processing.
This design significantly reduces sensor-side overhead,
although gateway performance may become a bottleneck in
large-scale deployments. Physical tampering,
denial-of-service, and side-channel attacks are outside the
scope of BVCRSA and should be mitigated using
complementary mechanisms such as secure boot,
tamper-resistant hardware, intrusion detection, and
hardware-assisted protections.

Fourth, BVCRSA verifies query results by cryptographically
binding authenticated searchable indexes, bitmap
commitments, aggregation entries, and blockchain-anchored
epoch states. However, it does not provide general
verifiable computation for bitmap evaluation or
homomorphic aggregation. Supporting such functionality
would require additional techniques, such as
zero-knowledge proofs, verifiable computation, or trusted
execution environments, at the cost of higher computation
and communication overhead.

Finally, BVCRSA follows the standard
searchable-encryption leakage model. The cloud observes
only the information explicitly defined by the formal
leakage profile, including query, search, and access
patterns required for efficient retrieval, while plaintext
records, reconstructed bitmaps, and cryptographic keys
remain protected. Nevertheless, repeated queries may still
permit limited inference about query selectivity or data
distribution. Reducing this inherent leakage through
techniques such as ORAM or differential privacy remains an
important direction for future work.

\section{Conclusion}

This paper presented \textbf{BVCRSA}, a blockchain-based
framework for verifiable conjunctive range search and
aggregation over encrypted IIoT data. BVCRSA integrates
attribute-based searchable encryption, authenticated
hierarchical range-cover indexing, protected bitmap
filtering, Threshold EC-ElGamal homomorphic aggregation,
and blockchain-assisted query-state verification into a
unified architecture. Unlike existing searchable
encryption schemes that primarily support secure
retrieval, BVCRSA jointly enables fine-grained access
control, conjunctive multi-range search,
privacy-preserving aggregation, threshold aggregate
decryption, and authenticated query-state verification.
Experimental results demonstrate efficient trapdoor
generation, practical bitmap-based query processing, low
verification overhead, and scalable index construction,
highlighting the practicality of BVCRSA for secure
outsourced IIoT analytics. Future work will investigate privacy-preserving indexed ABSE lookup
to avoid exhaustive token evaluation, forward-secure revocation with
epoch-evolving node keys, efficient dynamic index updates, and
distributed trust management. Further studies will evaluate BVCRSA
using large-scale real-world IIoT datasets and realistic end-to-end
deployments, while exploring post-quantum primitives to strengthen
its long-term security.

